WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.420
The problem with an applied science like exercise

2
00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.480
is that there are huge gaps.

3
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:05.700
When you're trying to extrapolate

4
00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.410
the literature into practice,

5
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.450
there are huge gaps.

6
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.360
And one of the things that the research reports

7
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.750
is a mean value, meaning an average.

8
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.220
So when I carry out a study,

9
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.560
let's say on high repetitions

10
00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.890
versus low repetitions,

11
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:19.930
I'm gonna report the averages.

12
00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.040
One group might gain 10%,

13
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.960
another group might gain 6%.

14
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.060
Within that, that does not explain

15
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:26.630
the inter-individual differences.

16
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:27.550
I'll see a,

17
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.240
if you look at a scatter plot

18
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.260
of the responses between individuals,

19
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.290
they're all over the place.

20
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.110
So people respond very differently to training.

21
00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.180
We can give general,

22
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:40.340
research can give us general guidelines to follow,

23
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.440
but then you need to take into account

24
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.060
your own experience

25
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.660
and your understanding of the research

26
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:47.810
because there are gaps.

27
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:49.920
You need to know how long was the study,

28
00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.220
what was the population studied,

29
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.190
how old were they,

30
00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.430
how trained or untrained were they.

31
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:57.340
There's so many factors that go into

32
00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.950
trying to extrapolate research into practice

33
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.280
and that really is where

34
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.020
your own personal experience and understanding,

35
00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.180
knowledge of the field comes into play.

36
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.520
And then you have to take into account

37
00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:10.570
the individual.

38
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.130
So you can use these as guidelines

39
00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:14.880
based on your understanding

40
00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.110
and what the research shows.

41
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.600
But then once you have someone

42
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:18.750
who you're working with,

43
00:01:18.980 --> 00:01:20.200
you need to be flexible enough

44
00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:21.400
and understanding enough

45
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.460
that he might not respond optimally

46
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:25.110
to what other people are.

47
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.310
The majority of people, the mean are,

48
00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:30.560
and then you have to be astute enough

49
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:35.140
to be able to manipulate the variables

50
00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.220
that you're looking at

51
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.870
to bring about a better effect at that point.

52
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.750
And that is a true evidence-based approach.

53
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:42.630
So it's taking research into account,

54
00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.380
having a good understanding yourself,

55
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.360
and then applying it to whoever it is

56
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:48.830
you're looking to apply it to.
